Home Assistant keeps every integration credential — passwords, API tokens — in
plain text inside .storage/core.config_entries. Any local user can read that
file, it travels inside every backup, and the gap has been open upstream since
November 2026 (home-assistant/core#191977, still unassigned).

Safe Store seals that field with AES-256-GCM before it reaches the disk and
opens it in memory at startup, so the integrations never notice. The file keeps
entry_id, domain and title readable; only the data is encrypted. Enabling it
takes one step in the interface — no YAML, no restart loop, no changes to any
integration.

The master key does not live in Home Assistant. A companion app holds it and
hands it over the local network with a token. It can come from a plain file or
from a SOPS-encrypted env file unlocked with an age identity that is
deliberately left out of backups, so a leaked backup contains ciphertext and
nothing else.

What it does not do is stated plainly: it cannot defend against root on the
machine that holds the key. A process that needs a secret in the clear to
authenticate cannot be protected by encrypting the file that sits next to it.
What Safe Store removes is the plaintext on disk and the plaintext inside
backups — the two ways these credentials actually leak.

